# Duran Duran’s Andy Taylor Dies at 65

> 🎵 *Curated Music Story from Pitchfork Official Publications*

### 📖 The Full Story

Newsletter Search Search News Reviews Best New Music Features Lists Columns Video Open Navigation Menu Menu Search Search News Duran Duran’s Andy Taylor Dies at 65 The guitarist was diagnosed with prostate cancer in 2018 By Hattie Lindert November 25, 2026 Andy Taylor in 1984 (Getty Images) Comment Loader Save Story Save this story Comment Loader Save Story Save this story Duran Duran guitarist Andy Taylor, who co-wrote hits like “Girls on Film” and “Hungry Like the Wolf,” died at home on Monday of prostate cancer. The band confirmed the news in a joint statement with his wife, Tracey, shared to their website. He was 65. Pitchfork has reached out to Duran Duran’s representatives for comment.

“Andy was there at the very beginning of our story,” the band wrote. “From the moment he arrived in Birmingham from the North East with a guitar and an unshakeable belief in what we could become, he brought an indelible fire and swagger to our music. His playing drove the songs that took us around the world, and with a dry wit that was matched only by his wonderfully unsentimental, no-nonsense outlook on life he kept us grounded and gave the band its edge through the extraordinary years that followed.”

Taylor joined Duran Duran in 1980, shortly after the band had begun booking live gigs at Birmingham club the Rum Runner. He had encountered the band’s ad in Melody Maker for a “live-wire guitarist.” When he arrived at the audition he met Nick Rhodes, bassist John Taylor, and drummer Roger Taylor, recounting in his 2008 memoir Wild Boy, “The three of us had a good laugh about the fact that by coincidence we all shared the same surname.” Simon Le Bon rounded out the lineup just a few months later, and the band signed to EMI in December, dropping their debut single, “Planet Earth,” in 1981.

### 🎤 Key Highlights & Community Reactions

The next two years saw Duran Duran become one of American MTV’s first success stories. The channel’s consistent airing of the videos for “Hungry Like the Wolf” and “Rio”—both of which Taylor co-wrote—earned the band a massive fanbase oversees, with some calling them the leaders of a “Second British Invasion.” They shared their third album, Seven and the Ragged Tiger , in 1983.

By 1984, a rift had emerged in Duran Duran, leading Andy and his bandmate John to form a new group, Power Station, with Robert Palmer and Chic drummer Tony Thompson. Although the supergroup didn’t last long, they recorded with Rod Stewart, Belinda Carlisle, and other big names during their active period. Duran Duran reunited in 1985 to perform at Live Aid in Philadelphia, but the show proved less of a reunion than a curtain call: Andy quit the band in 1986 to pursue a solo career that spawned two albums: 1987’s Thunder and 1990’s Dangerous . He also released a single, “Love or Liberation,” in 2019.

Although Andy rejoined Duran Duran during their 1993 comeback, the return proved short-lived, and neither John nor Roger took part in it. Andy stuck around for a 2001 reunion tour and the 2004 album Astronaut, but ultimately departed the group for a second time midway through recording an album in 2006. “Perhaps things might have been different if we all sat down together and discussed the tensions that tore us apart the first time around, but that never happened,” he wrote in Wild Boy . “There was no cocaine the second time around, but in the end, it was the similar tensions caused by internal disagreements that came bubbling back to the surface. History really did repeat itself.”

Andy’s illness was first publicized in 2022 at Duran Duran’s Rock Hall induction , when Le Bon read a letter from Andy explaining that his absence at the ceremony was due to a diagnosis of stage 4 metastatic prostate cancer he received in 2018. “I’m so very proud of these four brothers; I’m amazed at their durability, and I’m overjoyed at accepting this award,” he wrote of his bandmates. “I often doubted the day would come. I’m sure as hell glad I’m around to see the day.”

